# Alicloud Function Compute bundle — shared deps + the alibaba SDKs.
# Compiled to requirements-alicloud.txt. Nothing on the current branch
# reads this file (the alicloud deploy is a Phase 5 follow-up), but the
# dependency surface lives here so a future alicloud-deploy PR only has
# to flip LAMBDA_REQUIREMENTS_FILE / var.lambda_requirements to
# "requirements-alicloud.txt" without re-figuring-out which SDKs we
# depend on.
#
# `oss2` re-exports under both `oss2` and `alibabacloud_oss2`
# namespaces (the legacy short name is the only PyPI package). KMS /
# ECS use Alibaba's per-product SDK naming.
#
# `typing-extensions` is listed EXPLICITLY, even though it is already a
# transitive dependency of this file. `aiohttp` (pulled in by `oss2`)
# declares it as `typing_extensions>=4.4; python_version < "3.13"`, so
# it is only a dependency on the Python 3.12 runtime this bundle
# targets — see `runtime = "python3.12"` in
# infra/aws/modules/core/lambda.tf.
#
# Dependabot compiles this lock under Python >= 3.13, where that marker
# is false, so pip-compile concludes the dependency is unnecessary and
# DROPS the pin. The resulting lock then fails to install at all:
#
#     ERROR: In --require-hashes mode, all requirements must have
#     their versions pinned with ==. These do not:
#         typing_extensions>=4.4 ... (from aiohttp==3.14.5)
#
# (every requirement here carries a --hash, which puts pip in
# --require-hashes mode where transitive deps must be pinned with ==).
# Worse, the bundle would have been built without typing_extensions,
# and `import oss2` -> `import aiohttp` -> `import typing_extensions`
# would raise ImportError at cold start.
#
# Declaring it here makes the dependency unconditional, so the
# resolution is identical on 3.12 and 3.13+ and dependabot can no
# longer remove it. Do NOT drop this line — `scripts/
# check_requirements_lock.py` and tests/test_requirements_lock.py both
# guard it.
-r requirements.in

oss2
alibabacloud_kms20160120
alibabacloud_ecs20140526
typing-extensions
